\label{twist-def}
Let $\Gamma$ be a group and $S$ a $\Gamma$-graded semigroup. A \emph{Zhang twisting system}, or a \emph{Zhang twist, $\tau$ on $S$}, is a collection $\{\tau_{\alpha} \mid \alpha \in \Gamma\}$, where $\tau_{\alpha} : S \to S$ is a graded bijection for each $\alpha \in \Gamma$, and
\begin{equation} \label{twist-eq}
\tau_{\alpha}(s\tau_{\beta}(t)) = \tau_{\alpha}(s)\tau_{\alpha \beta}(t)
\end{equation}
for all $\alpha, \beta \in \Gamma$, $s \in S_{\beta}$, and $t \in S$. A Zhang twist $\tau = \{\tau_{\alpha} \mid \alpha \in \Gamma\}$ on $S$ is \emph{algebraic} if $\tau_{\alpha}$ is a semigroup automorphism and $\tau_{\alpha}\tau_{\beta} = \tau_{\alpha\beta}$, for all $\alpha, \beta \in \Gamma$.

\label{graphinsemigrp}
Recall that a \emph{directed graph} $E=(E^0,E^1,\mathbf{r},\mathbf{s})$ consists of two disjoint sets, $E^0$ and $E^1$ (containing \emph{vertices} and \emph{edges}, respectively), together with functions $\mathbf{s},\mathbf{r} : E^1 \to E^0$, called \emph{source} and \emph{range}, respectively. A \emph{path} $x$ in $E$ is a finite sequence of edges $x=e_1\cdots e_n$ such that $\mathbf{r}(e_i)=\mathbf{s}(e_{i+1})$ for all $i \in \{1,\dots,n-1\}$. In this case, $\mathbf{s}(x):=\mathbf{s}(e_1)$ is the \emph{source} of $x$, $\mathbf{r}(x):=\mathbf{r}(e_n)$ is the \emph{range} of $x$, and $|x|:=n$ is the \emph{length} of $x$. We view vertices as paths of length $0$, and extend $\mathbf{s}$ and $\mathbf{r}$ to $E^0$ by setting $\mathbf{s}(v) : = v$ and $\mathbf{r}(v) := v$ for all $v \in E^0$. We refer the reader to \cite{Abrams-Monograph} for background on directed graphs and associated algebras.

Given a directed graph $E=(E^0,E^1,\mathbf{r},\mathbf{s})$, the \emph{grqaph inverse semigroup $\mathcal{S}(E)$ of $E$} is the semigroup (with zero) generated by $E^0 \cup E^1 \cup \{e^{-1} \mid e\in E^1\}$, subject to the following relations for all $v,w\in E^0$ and $e,f\in E^1$ (with $\delta$ denoting the Kronecker delta):
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(V)] $vw = \delta_{v,w}v$;
\item[(E1)] $\mathbf{s}(e)e=e\mathbf{r}(e)=e$;
\item[(E2)] $\mathbf{r}(e)e^{-1}=e^{-1}\mathbf{s}(e)=e^{-1}$;
\item[(CK1)] $e^{-1}f=\delta _{e,f}\mathbf{r}(e)$.
\end{enumerate}
We define $v^{-1}=v$ for each $v \in E^0$, and for any path $y=e_1\cdots e_n$ ($e_1,\dots, e_n \in E^1$) we let $y^{-1} = e_n^{-1} \cdots e_1^{-1}$. With this notation, it is easy to see that every nonzero element of $\mathcal{S}(E)$ can be written as $xy^{-1}$, for some paths $x$ and $y$ satisfying $\mathbf{r}(x)=\mathbf{r}(y)$, and $\mathcal{S}(E)$ is indeed an inverse semigroup, with $(xy^{-1})^{-1} = yx^{-1}$. Moreover $\mathcal{S}(E)$ is naturally $\mathbb Z$-graded via $\phi : \mathcal{S}(E) \setminus \{0\} \to \mathbb Z$, where $\phi(xy^{-1}) := |x|-|y|$, for all paths $x$ and $y$ in $E$.

A \emph{graph automorphism} of $E$ is a pair of bijections $\phi_0 : E^0 \to E^0$ and $\phi_1 : E^1 \to E^1$, such that $\phi_0(\mathbf{s}(e)) = \mathbf{s}(\phi_1(e))$ and $\phi_0(\mathbf{r}(e)) = \mathbf{r}(\phi_1(e))$, for every $e \in E^1$. It is not hard to show (see, e.g.,~\cite[Theorem 20]{MM}) that any automorphism of $E$ can be extended to a, necessarily graded, automorphism of $\mathcal{S}(E)$, and hence also to a Zhang twist, as in Remark~\ref{aut-eg}.

Let $X$ be a nonempty set. Recall that a \emph{partial symmetry} of $X$ is a function $\phi: A\rightarrow B$, where $A,B \subseteq X$. Here we let $\operatorname{Dom}(\phi):=A$ and $\operatorname{Im}(\phi): = B$. The set $\mathcal{I}(X)$ of all partial symmetries of $X$ is an inverse semigroup with respect to composition of relations, called the \emph{symmetric inverse monoid}. Specifically, for all $\phi,\psi \in \mathcal{I}(X)$ we take $\phi \psi$ to be the composite of $\phi$ and $\psi$ as functions, restricted to the domain $\psi^{-1}(\operatorname{Im}(\psi)\cap \operatorname{Dom}(\phi))$. The empty function plays the role of the zero element in $\mathcal{I}(X)$.  

Next, let $\Gamma$ be a group, and let $\psi : X \rightarrow \Gamma$ a function. For each $\alpha \in \Gamma$ and nonempty $Y \subseteq X$, let $Y_{\alpha}:= Y\cap \psi^{-1}(\alpha)$ and
\begin{equation*}\label{mothercomp}
\mathcal{I}(X)_{\alpha}:= \big \{\phi \in \mathcal{I}(X) \, \big \vert  \, \phi(\operatorname{Dom}(\phi)_{\beta}) \subseteq X_{\alpha\beta} \text{ for all } \beta \in \Gamma \big \}. 
\end{equation*}
Then
\begin{equation*}\label{mothersemigroup}
\mathcal{I}^{\operatorname{gr}}(X):= \bigcup _{\alpha \in \Gamma} \mathcal{I}(X)_{\alpha} 
\end{equation*}
is a $\Gamma$-graded inverse semigroup, according to~\cite[Proposition 5.3]{RZ1}.

Suppose that $\rho : \Gamma \rightarrow \mathrm{Sym}(X)$ is a homomorphism such that $\rho(\alpha)(X_{\beta}) = X_{\beta}$ for all $\alpha, \beta \in \Gamma$, where $\mathrm{Sym}(X)$ denotes the group of all permutations of $X$. Then letting $\tau(\alpha)(\phi) := \rho(\alpha)\phi \rho(\alpha)^{-1}$, for each $\alpha \in \Gamma$ and $\phi \in \mathcal{I}^{\operatorname{gr}}(X)$, defines a homomorphism $\tau: \Gamma \rightarrow \mathrm{Aut}_{\mathrm{gr}}(\mathcal{I}^{\operatorname{gr}}(X))$, which leads to an algebraic Zhang twist on $\mathcal{I}^{\operatorname{gr}}(X)$, as in Remark~\ref{alg-eg}. 

To construct a concrete example of this sort, take $\Gamma$ to be any group, let $X := \bigsqcup_{\alpha \in \Gamma} X_{\alpha}$ be the disjoint union, where each $X_{\alpha}$ is a copy of $\Gamma$, and define $\rho(\alpha)(\gamma) := \alpha\gamma \in X_{\beta}$, for all $\alpha, \beta \in \Gamma$ and $\gamma \in X_{\beta}$.

\label{id-lem}
Let $\, \Gamma$ be a group, $S$ a $\, \Gamma$-graded semigroup, and $\tau = \{\tau_{\alpha} \mid \alpha \in \Gamma\}$ a Zhang twist on $S$. Then the following hold for all $\alpha, \beta \in \Gamma$, $s \in S_{\beta}$, and $t \in S$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[$(1)$] $\tau_{\alpha}(st) = \tau_{\alpha}(s)\tau_{\alpha \beta}\tau_{\beta}^{-1}(t)$;
\item[$(2)$] $\tau_{\alpha}^{-1}(s\tau_{\alpha\beta}(t)) = \tau_{\alpha}^{-1}(s)\tau_{\beta}(t)$;
\item[$(3)$] $\tau_{\alpha}^{-1}(st) = \tau_{\alpha}^{-1}(s)\tau_{\beta}\tau_{\alpha \beta}^{-1}(t)$.
\end{enumerate}

\label{twist-id-elt}
Let $\, \Gamma$ be a group, $S$ a $\, \Gamma$-graded semigroup, and $\tau = \{\tau_{\alpha} \mid \alpha \in \Gamma\}$ a Zhang twist on $S$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[$(1)$] For all $\alpha \in \Gamma$ we have $\tau_{\alpha}(0) = 0$, and hence $\, 0$ is the zero element of $S^{\tau}$.
\item[$(2)$] If $S$ is a monoid, then $\tau_{\varepsilon}(1) = 1$ if and only if $\tau_{\varepsilon}(s) = s$ for all $s \in S$.
\item[$(3)$] If $S$ is a monoid, then $\, 1 = \tau_{\alpha}^{-1}(1)\tau_{\varepsilon}(1)$ for all $\alpha \in \Gamma$. 
\item[$(4)$] If $S$ is an inverse semigroup, then $\tau_{\varepsilon}(u) = u$ for all $u \in E(S)$  if and only if $\tau_{\varepsilon}(s) = s$ for all $s \in S$.
\end{enumerate}

\label{inverse-twist}
Let $\, \Gamma$ be a group, $S$ a $\, \Gamma$-graded semigroup, and $\tau = \{\tau_{\alpha} \mid \alpha \in \Gamma\}$ a Zhang twist on $S$. 
\begin{enumerate}
\item[$(1)$] The set $\tau^{-1}: = \{\tau_{\alpha}^{-1} \mid \alpha \in \Gamma\}$ is a Zhang twist on $S^{\tau}$.
\item[$(2)$] For any Zhang twist $\sigma = \{\sigma_{\alpha} \mid \alpha \in \Gamma\}$ on $S^{\tau}$, the set $\tau\sigma := \{\tau_{\alpha}\sigma_{\alpha} \mid \alpha \in \Gamma\}$ is a Zhang twist on $S$, and $S^{\tau\sigma} = (S^{\tau})^{\sigma}$, as semigroups.
\end{enumerate}

\label{twist-reg}
Let $\, \Gamma$ be a group, $S$ a $\, \Gamma$-graded semigroup, and $\tau = \{\tau_{\alpha} \mid \alpha \in \Gamma\}$ a Zhang twist on $S$. 
\begin{enumerate}
\item[$(1)$] $S$ is regular if and only if $S^{\tau}$ is.
\item[$(2)$] $S$ is an inverse semigroup if and only if $S^{\tau}$ is.
\end{enumerate}

\label{npt}
Let $\, \Gamma$ be a group, $S$ a $\, \Gamma$-graded inverse semigroup, and $\tau = \{\tau_{\alpha} \mid \alpha \in \Gamma\}$ a Zhang twist on $S$, such that $\tau_{\varepsilon}(p)=p$ for all $p \in S$. Also let $\alpha \in \Gamma$, $s \in S_{\alpha}$, and $r,t \in S$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[$(1)$] The inner inverse of $s$ in $S^{\tau}$ is $\tau_{\alpha}^{-1}(s^{-1})$.
\item[$(2)$] If $\tau$ is algebraic, then $\tau_{\alpha}^{-1}(s^{-1})=\tau_{\alpha^{-1}}(s)^{-1}$.
\item[$(3)$] Letting $\leq$, respectively $\leq^{\tau}$, denote the natural partial order on $S$, respectively $S^{\tau}$, we have $\leq \, = \, \leq^{\tau}$.
\item[$(4)$] We have $r \leq t$ if and only if $\tau_{\alpha}(r) \leq \tau_{\alpha}(t)$.
\item[$(5)$] We have $\tau_{\alpha}(t)\tau_{\alpha}(t)^{-1} = \tau_{\alpha}(tt^{-1})$.
\item[$(6)$] Suppose that $t \in S_{\alpha}$. Then $s$ and $t$ are compatible, respectively orthogonal, in $S$ if and only if they are compatible, respectively orthogonal, in $S^{\tau}$.
\end{enumerate}

\label{bool-def}
Let $\Gamma$ be a group, and $S$ a $\Gamma$-graded inverse semigroup. Then $S$ is $\Gamma$-\emph{graded-Boolean}, or simply \emph{graded-Boolean}, if it satisfies the following conditions.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[$(1)$] For all $\alpha \in \Gamma$, every orthogonal pair $s,t \in S_{\alpha}$ has a join $s\vee t$ in $S$.

\smallskip 

\item[$(2)$] $E(S)$ is a distributive lattice, and every nonzero principal order ideal of $E(S)$ is a Boolean algebra.
\end{enumerate}

\label{bool-prop}
Let $\, \Gamma$ be a group, $S$ a $\, \Gamma$-graded inverse semigroup, and $\tau = \{\tau_{\alpha} \mid \alpha \in \Gamma\}$ a Zhang twist on $S$. 
\begin{enumerate}
\item[$(1)$] $S$ is an inverse $\land$-semigroup if and only if $S^{\tau}$ is.

\item[$(2)$] $S$ is a $\, \Gamma$-graded-Boolean inverse semigroup if and only if $S^{\tau}$ is.
\end{enumerate}

Let $S$ be a monoid, $S\langle x, y \rangle$ the monoid freely generated by $S$, $x$, and $y$ (i.e., the free product of $S$ and $\langle x, y \rangle$),  and $f : S\rightarrow uSu$ a semigroup isomorphism, where $u \in E(S)$ and $f(1)=u$. Define
\begin{align}\label{skewcornerla}
S[x,y,f] : = S\langle x, y \rangle / \langle yx=1, xy=u, xs=f(s)x, sy=yf(s) \mid s\in S\rangle. 
\end{align}
We refer to $uSu$ as a \emph{corner monoid}, and to $S[x,y,f]$ as a \emph{corner skew monoid}.

\label{chathapy}
Let $S$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-graded monoid, with $t\in S_{-1}$ and $s\in S_1$ such that $ts=1$. Then $S\cong S_0[x,y,f]$ as graded semigroups, where 
\begin{align*}
f : S_0 &\longrightarrow st \, S_0 \, st\\
 r &\longmapsto s\, r\, t.
\end{align*}

Let $S[x,y,f]$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-graded corner skew monoid, and set $u:= f(1)$.  
\begin{enumerate}
\item[$(1)$] $S$ is an inverse semigroup if and only if $S[x,y,f]$ is. 
\item[$(2)$] $S[x,y,f]$ is strongly $\mathbb{Z}$-graded if and only if $SuS=S$. 
 \end{enumerate}

\label{iso-twist}
Let $\, \Gamma$ be a group, and let $S$ and $T$ be $\, \Gamma$-graded semigroups. Then the following are equivalent.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[$(1)$] $T \cong S^{\tau}$ as graded semigroups, for some Zhang twist $\tau$ on $S$.
\item[$(2)$] For each $\alpha \in \Gamma$ there is a graded bijection $\phi_{\alpha} : T \to S$, such that $\phi_{\alpha}(t_1t_2) = \phi_{\alpha}(t_1)\phi_{\alpha\beta}(t_2)$ for all $\alpha, \beta \in \Gamma$, $t_1 \in T_{\beta}$, and $t_2 \in T$.
\end{enumerate}

\label{groupoid-twist-def}
Let $\Gamma$ be a group and $\mathcal{G}$ a $\Gamma$-graded ample groupoid. A \emph{Zhang twisting system}, or a \emph{Zhang twist, $\tau$ on $\mathcal{G}$}, is a collection $\{\tau_{\alpha} \mid \alpha \in \Gamma\}$, where each $\tau_{\alpha} : \mathcal{G} \to \mathcal{G}$ is a graded homeomorphism (i.e., $\phi(\mathcal{G}_{\alpha}) \subseteq \mathcal{G}_{\alpha}$ for all $\alpha \in \Gamma$), such that the following conditions are satisfied for all $\alpha, \beta \in \Gamma$:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(T1)] $\tau_{\alpha} (\mathcal G^0)\subseteq \mathcal G^0$;
\item[(T2)] $\tau_{\alpha}|_{\mathcal G^0}\tau_{\beta}|_{\mathcal G^0} = \tau_{\alpha\beta}|_{\mathcal G^0}$;
\item[(T3)] $\bm{d}\circ \tau_{\alpha}=\tau_{\alpha}\circ \bm{d}$;
\item[(T4)] $\bm{r}\circ \tau_{\alpha}=\tau_{\alpha}\circ \bm{r}$;
\item[(T5)] if $a\in \mathcal G_{\beta}$ and $b \in \mathcal{G}$ are such that $\bm{d}(a)=\bm{r}(\tau_{\beta}(b))$, then $\tau_{\alpha}(a\tau_{\beta}(b)) = \tau_{\alpha}(a)\tau_{\alpha\beta}(b)$.
\end{itemize}

\label{p:tau.eps}
Let $\, \Gamma$ be a group, $\mathcal{G}$ a $\Gamma$-graded ample groupoid, and $\tau = \{\tau_{\alpha} \mid \alpha \in \Gamma\}$ a Zhang twist on $\mathcal{G}$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[$(1)$] $\tau_\varepsilon=1_{\mathcal G}$, the identity functor on $\mathcal{G}$.
\item[$(2)$] Each $\tau_\alpha$ restricts to a functor on $\mathcal{G}_\varepsilon$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $\Gamma$ be a group, $S$ a $\Gamma$-graded inverse semigroup, and $\tau = \{\tau_{\alpha} \mid \alpha \in \Gamma\}$ a Zhang twist on $S$. Then $\tau$ is \emph{semi-algebraic} if it satisfies the following conditions.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[$(1)$] For all $u \in E(S)$, we have $\tau_{\varepsilon}(u) = u$.
\item[$(2)$] For all $\alpha \in \Gamma$ and $s \in S$, we have $\tau_{\alpha}^{-1}(s^{-1})= \tau_{\alpha^{-1}}(s)^{-1}$.
\item[$(3)$] The set $\{\tau_{\alpha}|_{E(S)} \mid \alpha \in \Gamma\}$ is an algebraic Zhang twist on $E(S)$.
\end{enumerate}

\label{duality-thrm}
Let $\, \Gamma$ be a group, $S$ a $\, \Gamma$-graded-Boolean inverse $\land$-semigroup, and $\mathcal{G}$ a $\, \Gamma$-graded Hausdorff ample groupoid.
